css:
/* The step rule, kept but quietened: with every step centred, a heavy
   left bar competes with the alignment it used to provide. */
.subj-maths .dm-step { text-align:center; }

/* `इति सिद्धम्` — the end of a proof.
   The reference boxes it, and it earns that: it is the one line in an
   answer that says the argument is complete. Plain bold text among four
   other bold phrases said nothing. */
.qed { display:inline-block; font-weight:700; color:#123a5e;
      background:#eef3fb; border:1.4px solid #c3d6ea; border-radius:7px;
      padding:3px 12px; margin:4px 0; }

/* An equation number sits at the END of its step, not adrift on the next
   line. */
.subj-maths .dm>.k { margin-left:10px; white-space:nowrap; }

/* LARGER MATHS, AND A FLOOR UNDER THE SMALL PARTS.
   Maths is the subject where the notation IS the content. Worse, it
   NESTS: a matrix cell holds a fraction, and that fraction can hold
   another. Each level multiplies, and at the old sizes a nested
   fraction inside a matrix inside a column came out at 13.3px —
   which is 9.8px on paper once PRINT_ZOOM (0.734) is applied, below
   the 11px legibility floor. The gate did not catch it because it
   measures the unzoomed size, where 13.3px passes.
   Sized so the SMALLEST thing on the page still clears the floor:
   18 x .95 x .97 = 16.6px, which prints at 12.2px. */
.subj-maths .dm { font-size:19px; }
.subj-maths .acol .dm { font-size:17.5px; }
.subj-maths .mx>.mxg>span { font-size:19px; }
.subj-maths .acol .mx>.mxg>span { font-size:18px; }
/* Gentler steps for the nested levels, for the same reason. */
.subj-maths .fr { font-size:.95em; }
.subj-maths .fr .fr { font-size:.97em; }

/* MORE AIR BETWEEN THE BOXES.
   Eleven boxed results stacked at the default spacing read as one
   striped block; the gap is what makes each a separate fact. */
.subj-maths .fcard>.frow { margin:7px 0; padding-bottom:7px; }
.subj-maths .fcard { padding:14px 16px; }

/* A MATRIX MUST NOT RUN OFF THE PAGE.
   A 3x3 whose cells hold `cos x cos y - sin x sin y + 0` is wider
   than a 440px column, and `nowrap` on the cells meant it simply
   extended past the sheet edge — the right-hand columns were cut
   off in print with no error, because the page's scrollHeight never
   grew. Letting a long cell wrap keeps every entry on the paper;
   the grid stays aligned because the cells are still grid items. */
.subj-maths .mx { max-width:100%; }
.subj-maths .mx>.mxg { max-width:100%; }
.subj-maths .mx>.mxg>span { white-space:normal;
      overflow-wrap:break-word; }

/* The dense step has to out-rank `.subj-maths .acol .mx>.mxg>span`,
   which is four classes deep and sets 18px. Same shape plus the
   marker class, so it wins on specificity rather than !important. */
.subj-maths .mx.mx-dense>.mxg>span { font-size:16px; }
.subj-maths .acol .mx.mx-dense>.mxg>span { font-size:16px; }
